    <section id="m01-introduction-to-net-cli-clr-framework-architecture-02" class="mb-5">
        <h2>Introduction to .NET, CLI, CLR, Framework Architecture</h2>
        <p class="zoomable">The <span class="tt" data-tip="Command Line Interface used to create, build, and run .NET projects">CLI</span> lets you create projects with a single command.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">The <span class="tt" data-tip="Common Language Runtime that executes your compiled C# code">CLR</span> loads your app, manages memory, and catches unsafe actions.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">The Base Class Library gives ready-made pieces like collections, JSON helpers, and networking.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">The architecture separates tools, runtime, libraries, and your code so each part stays simple.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">Knowing this stack helps you debug faster because you know where to look.</p>
        <p class="text-success fw-semibold zoomable"><strong>Why this matters?</strong> Understanding the layers prevents mystery errors and speeds up your build-measure-learn loop.</p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded zoomable">dotnet new console -> CLI creates files
dotnet build         -> CLI calls compiler
dotnet run           -> CLR executes IL</pre>

        <figure class="zoomable text-center">
            <svg width="360" height="160" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="CLI to CLR flow">
                <rect x="10" y="20" width="90" height="40" fill="#cff4fc" stroke="#0dcaf0" />
                <text x="25" y="45" fill="#0d6efd">CLI</text>
                <rect x="130" y="20" width="90" height="40" fill="#e2e3e5" stroke="#6c757d" />
                <text x="140" y="45" fill="#6c757d">Compiler</text>
                <rect x="250" y="20" width="90" height="40" fill="#d1e7dd" stroke="#198754" />
                <text x="265" y="45" fill="#198754">CLR</text>
                <line x1="100" y1="40" x2="130" y2="40" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
                <line x1="220" y1="40" x2="250" y2="40" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
                <rect x="130" y="90" width="210" height="50" fill="#f8d7da" stroke="#dc3545" />
                <text x="140" y="120" fill="#dc3545">Your C# Project &amp; Libraries</text>
                <defs>
                    <marker id="arrow" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="5" refY="5" orient="auto">
                        <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="#0d6efd" />
                    </marker>
                </defs>
            </svg>
            <figcaption class="small">Commands move through compiler into the CLR that runs your code.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="p-3 example-box rounded zoomable">
            <strong>Example:</strong> Run <code>dotnet new console</code> to generate Program.cs and the project file without writing boilerplate.
        </div>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded zoomable"><code>// Inspect CLI info
Console.WriteLine($"CLR Version: {Environment.Version}");
Console.WriteLine($"Base Dir: {AppContext.BaseDirectory}");</code></pre>

        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Real-world use case:</strong> When a build server fails, you can tell whether the issue is the CLI command, the compiler, or the CLR environment.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Keep a cheat sheet of common dotnet commands near you.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Advanced note:</strong> IL (Intermediate Language) is just-in-time compiled differently on Windows, Linux, and macOS, but your C# code stays the same.</p>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-3">
            <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="#m01-course-fundamentals-and-net-overview-01">Prev</a>
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#m01-introducing-visual-studio-03">Next</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="m01-introducing-visual-studio-03" class="mb-5">
        <h2>Introducing Visual Studio</h2>
        <p class="zoomable"><span class="tt" data-tip="Microsoft's full IDE with templates, designer tools, and debugger">Visual Studio</span> is your friendly workspace.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">It creates projects, restores packages, and provides IntelliSense hints while you type.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">The Solution Explorer shows all files so you never lose track.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">The debugger highlights lines, lets you inspect variables, and pauses errors safely.</p>
        <p class="text-success fw-semibold zoomable"><strong>Why this matters?</strong> A comfortable tool speeds learning and reduces setup friction.</p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded zoomable">Toolbar -> Create New Project -> Console App
Solution Explorer -> Program.cs
Run (F5) -> Output window shows "Hello"</pre>

        <figure class="zoomable text-center">
            <svg width="340" height="140" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="IDE panels">
                <rect x="10" y="20" width="120" height="100" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#f59e0b" />
                <text x="20" y="70" fill="#b45309">Solution Explorer</text>
                <rect x="150" y="20" width="180" height="60" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#0ea5e9" />
                <text x="170" y="55" fill="#0369a1">Editor</text>
                <rect x="150" y="90" width="180" height="30" fill="#e2e3e5" stroke="#6c757d" />
                <text x="180" y="110" fill="#6c757d">Output</text>
            </svg>
            <figcaption class="small">Key Visual Studio panels you will use.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="p-3 example-box rounded zoomable">
            <strong>Example:</strong> Press <kbd>Ctrl + .</kbd> to trigger quick fixes when Visual Studio suggests a namespace import.
        </div>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded zoomable"><code>// Quick demo: set a breakpoint and inspect variables
int number = 5;
int doubled = number * 2;
Console.WriteLine($"Doubled value: {doubled}");
// In Visual Studio, watch 'doubled' while stepping over.</code></pre>

        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Real-world use case:</strong> Debugging a failing API call is faster when you can watch variables and HTTP responses.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Keep the Error List window open; it often shows fixes you can click.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Advanced note:</strong> Visual Studio can attach to running processes for live debugging of background services.</p>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-3">
            <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="#m01-introduction-to-net-cli-clr-framework-architecture-02">Prev</a>
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#m01-c-language-introduction-04">Next</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="m01-c-language-introduction-04" class="mb-5">
        <h2>C# Language Introduction</h2>
        <p class="zoomable">C# is an object-oriented language that feels readable even when you are new.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">Its type system keeps mistakes low while still being flexible with <span class="tt" data-tip="Implicit typing uses the var keyword when the compiler can infer the type">var</span> and generics.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">You write classes to bundle data and behavior together.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">Methods describe actions, while properties describe state with simple getters and setters.</p>
        <p class="text-success fw-semibold zoomable"><strong>Why this matters?</strong> Knowing the building blocks lets you read and write C# code without guessing.</p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded zoomable">Class -> Properties -> Methods -> Objects
Object -> Interact -> Console / Web / Database</pre>

        <figure class="zoomable text-center">
            <svg width="340" height="150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Class to object flow">
                <rect x="20" y="40" width="90" height="60" fill="#e7f5ff" stroke="#0d6efd" />
                <text x="30" y="75" fill="#0d6efd">Class</text>
                <rect x="140" y="20" width="90" height="40" fill="#d1e7dd" stroke="#198754" />
                <text x="150" y="45" fill="#198754">Properties</text>
                <rect x="140" y="90" width="90" height="40" fill="#ffe5d9" stroke="#fd7e14" />
                <text x="155" y="115" fill="#d9480f">Methods</text>
                <rect x="260" y="60" width="70" height="40" fill="#f8d7da" stroke="#dc3545" />
                <text x="270" y="85" fill="#dc3545">Object</text>
                <line x1="110" y1="70" x2="140" y2="40" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
                <line x1="110" y1="70" x2="140" y2="110" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
                <line x1="230" y1="40" x2="260" y2="80" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
                <line x1="230" y1="110" x2="260" y2="80" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
            </svg>
            <figcaption class="small">From class design to real objects you use in code.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="p-3 example-box rounded zoomable">
            <strong>Example:</strong> Define a <span class="tt" data-tip="A class is a blueprint for creating objects with the same shape">class</span> named <em>Book</em> with Title and Author, then print them.
        </div>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded zoomable"><code>public class Book
{
    public string Title { get; set; }
    public string Author { get; set; }
}

var book = new Book { Title = "C# Joy", Author = "Tech Genie" };
Console.WriteLine($"{book.Title} by {book.Author}");</code></pre>

        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Real-world use case:</strong> Modeling a shopping cart as C# classes makes it easy to add features like discounts later.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Start with one class and one method; test it before adding more.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Advanced note:</strong> Modern C# supports records and pattern matching, giving you terse yet expressive code.</p>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-3">
            <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="#m01-introducing-visual-studio-03">Prev</a>
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#m01-c-language-tokens-versions-syntax-structure-05">Next</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="m01-c-language-tokens-versions-syntax-structure-05" class="mb-5">
        <h2>C# Language Tokens, Versions, Syntax structure</h2>
        <p class="zoomable">Tokens are the smallest parts of code: keywords, identifiers, operators, and separators.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">Every C# file follows a clear structure: using statements, namespace, class, members.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">New language versions add handy features like <span class="tt" data-tip="A shorter way to declare classes for immutable data">records</span> and improved pattern matching.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">Consistent formatting makes your code readable even to beginners.</p>
        <p class="text-success fw-semibold zoomable"><strong>Why this matters?</strong> Understanding tokens helps you decode compiler messages quickly.</p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded zoomable">using System;   // Directive
namespace Demo   // Namespace token
{ class Greeter { } }</pre>

        <figure class="zoomable text-center">
            <svg width="340" height="130" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Token map">
                <rect x="20" y="20" width="70" height="40" fill="#e2e3e5" stroke="#6c757d" />
                <text x="30" y="45" fill="#6c757d">using</text>
                <rect x="110" y="20" width="70" height="40" fill="#d1e7dd" stroke="#198754" />
                <text x="120" y="45" fill="#198754">class</text>
                <rect x="200" y="20" width="70" height="40" fill="#ffe5d9" stroke="#fd7e14" />
                <text x="210" y="45" fill="#d9480f">method</text>
                <rect x="290" y="20" width="40" height="40" fill="#cff4fc" stroke="#0dcaf0" />
                <text x="297" y="45" fill="#0d6efd">;</text>
                <line x1="55" y1="60" x2="55" y2="100" stroke="#0d6efd" />
                <line x1="145" y1="60" x2="145" y2="100" stroke="#0d6efd" />
                <line x1="235" y1="60" x2="235" y2="100" stroke="#0d6efd" />
            </svg>
            <figcaption class="small">Tokens form the readable structure of your file.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="p-3 example-box rounded zoomable">
            <strong>Example:</strong> Identify tokens in <code>int score = 10;</code> (keyword <em>int</em>, identifier <em>score</em>, operator <em>=</em>, literal <em>10</em>, separator <em>;</em>).
        </div>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded zoomable"><code>using System;

namespace TokenDemo
{
    public class Greeter
    {
        public void SayHi()
        {
            string message = "Hi";
            Console.WriteLine(message);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Real-world use case:</strong> Code reviews become smoother when everyone follows the same syntax structure.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> Run code formatters (like Visual Studio's Ctrl+K, Ctrl+D) to clean up spacing.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Advanced note:</strong> The Roslyn compiler exposes syntax trees so tools can analyze or auto-fix your code.</p>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-3">
            <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="#m01-c-language-introduction-04">Prev</a>
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#m01-first-c-application-visual-studio-setup-console-cl-06">Next</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="m01-first-c-application-visual-studio-setup-console-cl-06" class="mb-5">
        <h2>First C# Application (Visual Studio setup, Console class)</h2>
        <p class="zoomable">You will create a console app, build it, and see output in seconds.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">The <span class="tt" data-tip="A static class that lets you read and write text in the terminal window">Console</span> class provides easy methods like WriteLine and ReadLine.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">Running from Visual Studio or the CLI uses the same compiled code.</p>
        <p class="zoomable">This first win shows the full loop: write, build, run, observe.</p>
        <p class="text-success fw-semibold zoomable"><strong>Why this matters?</strong> Quick feedback builds confidence and keeps motivation high.</p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded zoomable">dotnet new console -o HelloWorld
cd HelloWorld
dotnet run  // prints Hello World!</pre>

        <figure class="zoomable text-center">
            <svg width="360" height="140" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Build and run flow">
                <rect x="20" y="40" width="80" height="60" fill="#d1e7dd" stroke="#198754" />
                <text x="35" y="75" fill="#198754">Code</text>
                <rect x="130" y="40" width="80" height="60" fill="#e2e3e5" stroke="#6c757d" />
                <text x="145" y="75" fill="#6c757d">Build</text>
                <rect x="240" y="40" width="80" height="60" fill="#cff4fc" stroke="#0dcaf0" />
                <text x="260" y="75" fill="#0d6efd">Run</text>
                <line x1="100" y1="70" x2="130" y2="70" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
                <line x1="210" y1="70" x2="240" y2="70" stroke="#0d6efd" marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
            </svg>
            <figcaption class="small">Build then run to see your program respond.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="p-3 example-box rounded zoomable">
            <strong>Example:</strong> Ask a user for their name and greet them using <code>Console.ReadLine()</code> and <code>Console.WriteLine()</code>.
        </div>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded zoomable"><code>using System;

Console.Write("What is your name? ");
string? name = Console.ReadLine();
Console.WriteLine($"Hello, {name}! Welcome to C# New.");</code></pre>

        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Real-world use case:</strong> Many internal tools start as console apps before growing into services.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Beginner tip:</strong> If the console window closes too fast, add <code>Console.ReadKey()</code> at the end during learning.</p>
        <p class="zoomable"><strong>Advanced note:</strong> Console apps can host background services or minimal APIs using the same Program.cs file.</p>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-3">
            <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="#m01-c-language-tokens-versions-syntax-structure-05">Prev</a>
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#next-subtopic">Next</a>
        </div>
    </section>

